# Code owners for Praxis.
#
# Praxis currently has one maintainer, so this file does not distribute review
# load — it exists so that GitHub requests a review automatically, so ownership
# is explicit if that changes, and so the paths where a careless change is
# expensive are named out loud rather than remembered.
#
# Syntax: https://docs.github.com/en/repositories/managing-your-repositorys-settings-and-features/customizing-your-repository/about-code-owners
# Last matching pattern wins.

# Default owner for everything in the repository.
*                       @akansh23-cloud

# --- Paths where review is load-bearing ---------------------------------
#
# These are listed separately from the catch-all above. Today they resolve to
# the same person; when a second maintainer joins, this is where the split
# should start, because these are the files where a plausible-looking change
# can quietly remove a guarantee.

# The API types are the security boundary. The closed action vocabulary
# (ADR-001), spec immutability (ADR-002) and the discriminated-union CEL rules
# are enforced here and nowhere else — weakening a marker weakens the system
# with no test necessarily failing.
/api/                   @akansh23-cloud

# Generated from the markers above. Reviewed as evidence that a types change
# was regenerated, never edited by hand.
/config/crd/            @akansh23-cloud
/config/rbac/           @akansh23-cloud

# The privilege split (analyzer: no write RBAC; executor: no egress) is only
# real if these manifests say so.
/config/network-policy/ @akansh23-cloud

# The policy bundle is the final authority on what Praxis may do, and there is
# no bypass path anywhere in the codebase.
/policies/              @akansh23-cloud

# Architecture decisions and the plan the phases are contracted against.
/docs/                  @akansh23-cloud

# Supply chain: what CI runs, and what the hooks let through.
/.github/               @akansh23-cloud
/.pre-commit-config.yaml @akansh23-cloud
/Makefile               @akansh23-cloud
